<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reginarids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Reginar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reginarids were a noble family of Lotharingia descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginar-duke-of-lorraine">Reginar Longneck</a> (died 915), who held counties in Hainaut and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a>. In the 10th century they fought the East Frankish kings for power in Lotharingia and were repeatedly exiled. Their descendants became the counts of Hainaut and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, the latter later dukes of Brabant.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reginarids.jpg" alt="Reginarids" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan, Avalokitesvara, Ssolbergj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Belgium.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 898 Reginar Longneck, the most powerful noble of Lotharingia and lay abbot of several great monasteries, held comital authority there, and his descendants, the <b>Reginarids</b>, treated Hainaut as their family seat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1003 Lambert I the Bearded, a member of the <b>Reginarid</b> family, took the title of count of Louvain.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-louvain">County of Louvain</a></li><li><blockquote>Local nobles, led by the <b>Reginarids</b>, repeatedly switched allegiance between East and West Francia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lotharingia">Duchy of Lotharingia</a></li></ul></section>
